<section xml:id="sec-mod1-orientation">
    <title>Module 1: Mathematical Language and Proof Strategies</title>

    <objectives xml:id="obj-mod1">
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Correctly read and write mathematical statements using
            <m>\forall</m> and <m>\exists</m>, including statements
            with multiple quantifiers.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Form the negation of a quantified statement, applying De
            Morgan's laws and quantifier-negation rules accurately.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Identify the hypothesis and conclusion of an implication, and
            correctly state its converse and contrapositive.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Choose and execute an appropriate proof strategy (direct proof,
            contrapositive, contradiction, or induction) for a given claim.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Write proofs that are clear, complete, and in grammatically
            correct mathematical prose.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Read a short passage from Bauldry and identify the logical
            structure of its definitions and theorems.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </objectives>

    <p>Orientation</p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->

        <p>
          Welcome to the first module of <em>Intensive Analysis Prep</em>.
          Whether you are refreshing skills from a real analysis course
          taken years ago, or building on a proof-based course from a
          different area of mathematics, this module starts in the same
          place for everyone: the language mathematicians use to make
          precise statements, and the strategies they use to prove them.
        </p>
        <p>
          The self-assessment questions below are <alert>not graded</alert>.
          They are designed to help you locate yourself within the module
          and flag concepts that may need extra attention. Write informally;
          a sentence or two per question is fine.
        </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sa-1">
        <title>Quantifiers</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Consider the two statements:
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                <m>\forall x \in \R \;\; \exists n \in \N
                \text{ such that } x \lt n</m>
              </li>
              <li>
                <m>\exists n \in \N \text{ such that }
                \forall x \in \R,\;\; x \lt n</m>
              </li>
            </ol>
            In plain English, what does each statement say?
            Which is true and which is false?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sa-2">
        <title>Negation</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Write the negation of:
            <q>For every <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> there exists
            <m>\delta \gt 0</m> such that <m>|x - a| \lt \delta</m>
            implies <m>|f(x) - L| \lt \varepsilon</m>.</q>
          </p>
          <p>
            (This is the definition of <m>\lim_{x \to a} f(x) = L</m>.
            Its negation is the statement that the limit does
            <em>not</em> equal <m>L</m>.)
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sa-3">
        <title>Proof Strategies</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            For each claim, identify whether you would first try
            direct proof, proof by contrapositive, or proof by
            contradiction&#x2014;and say briefly why.
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>If <m>n^2</m> is odd, then <m>n</m> is odd.</li>
              <li>There are infinitely many prime numbers.</li>
              <li>
                If <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are rational, then
                <m>a + b</m> is rational.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>


    <!-- ============================================================ -->

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sa-4">
        <title>Proof by Induction</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Recall that a proof by induction on <m>n</m> requires two steps.
            Name them and state briefly what each requires you to show.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sa-5">
        <title>Your Background</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Briefly describe your proof-writing background. Have you taken
            a formal proofs course (like one using Hirst)? A real analysis
            course? Neither? How long ago? What felt most comfortable, and
            what felt hardest?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod1-instructor-note">
        <title>A Note from the Instructor</title>
        <p>
          Graduate real analysis is hard because it demands two things
          simultaneously: mathematical content (what is true) and logical
          precision (why it must be true, expressed unambiguously). Most
          students find the content familiar&#x2014;you have seen sequences,
          limits, and derivatives before. The challenge in MAT 5610 is the
          second part: Bauldry's definitions are short but dense, and every
          word is load-bearing.
        </p>
        <p>
          This module builds the foundation for that precision. The
          quantifiers <m>\forall</m> and <m>\exists</m> will appear in
          virtually every definition we encounter this summer. The proof
          strategies covered here are the exact strategies Bauldry's proofs
          rely on, often without naming them. If you breeze through the
          self-assessment above, spend your time on the Bridge Reading and
          the harder practice problems. If the self-assessment reveals gaps,
          work through all the materials carefully. Either way, do not skip
          the Worked Examples.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod1-pacing">
        <title>A Note on Pacing</title>
        <p><alert>A note on pacing.</alert> This module covers material that is foundational for everything that follows, so invest time here even if the topics feel familiar. <em>If you have prior real analysis experience:</em> you may move quickly through the Study Guide and Worked Examples, but spend extra time on the Bridge Reading&#x2014;the goal is not just to recall proof strategies but to see how Bauldry deploys them in a graduate-level context. <em>If your background is in other proof-based courses (but not analysis):</em> work through all components carefully. The quantifier-negation exercises and the induction worked example are especially important; do not skip the Practice Problem Set.</p>
      </paragraphs>

